;; The world seat: plays the outside world an org's brief implies, one
;; simulated day per firing, for seven days. It only ever sits on a
;; clone (docs/spec-demo-clones.md): its rows are the week the film
;; shows before the person merges the org. Epic cafa6106, M3c.
{:template
 {:params {:brief "The org_plan's brief, the one paragraph the person typed: what this business is and does."
           :kinds "The org's kinds whose create doors the outside world knocks on, by name, comma-separated: every kind whose create door a non-staff cast member's :scope holds in the seed (the request a customer files, the record a supplier sends, the payment a payer makes). Scope and :delivers get one create entry per kind. A seed that still gives :kind alone fills it as a one-element :kinds."
           :cast "The ids of the cast members the world plays, as the seed's :cast gives them, comma-separated: the customers, suppliers and callers whose words the rows carry. Never the org's own people."
           :day_one "The date day 1 is, as the clone's seed resolves [:days 0] (YYYY-MM-DD). Day k is day_one plus k-1 days."
           :domain_id "The id of the org's domain row: the seat's :domain ref."}
  :lists [:kinds]
  :aliases {:kind :kinds}
  :note "Seat this only in a clone's seed, never in an org that is live: it writes rows a real customer did not file. Seven firings make the week; an eighth finds day 7 already written and does nothing. The brief opens :instructions, not :charter (whose cap is 1200): :instructions holds 2000 characters, and an org_plan's brief may hold 4000, so a brief past about 1200 characters still overflows it. Nothing truncates it; conform's seed check names the field and its length."}
 :seat
 {:name "world"
  :mode "fired"
  :rows_per_firing 1
  :cadence_seconds 3600
  :fire_interval_seconds 300
  :max_open_sittings 1
  :standing_ttl_seconds 604800
  :budget_usd_per_week 5M
  :sitting_budget_tokens 80000
  :sitting_idle_seconds 1800
  :keep_transcripts "fired"
  :transcript_days 30
  :acts_as_cast true
  :domain "{{domain_id}}"
  :charter "You play the outside world for one business across seven simulated days; its brief is in your instructions. Each firing is one day. Write that day's 1 to 3 rows, each in the words of one cast member and made as that member, with the day's date as today. Name each row the way its sender would on the phone (Maya's first order, Maya's dispute, Tuesday's payout): a provider or reference id goes in its own field, never in the field a row's summary or title reads. The week has an arc: on day 2 something goes wrong that the brief implies (a record is late, a request is too thin, a payer disputes); on day 5 the outside world answers the house's chase or escalation; on day 7 it brings what finishes a job (the last record, a payment). Other days stay ordinary. A refusal is part of the week the film shows, not an error: never retry it to make it pass, never change a row after you create it, and never take a door the org's own seats hold; they answer."
  :instructions "The business: {{brief}}\n\nYou sit in `world`. For your one day: list the rows you created of each kind in {{kinds}} (waymark_query with that kind, return \"summary\"); the next day is one past the latest they carry, day 1 on {{day_one}}, day k on {{day_one}} plus k-1 days; after day 7, do nothing. Then create that day's rows with waymark_invoke, kind one of {{kinds}}, action \"create\", no id, and `as` the cast member ({{cast}}) whose words it carries. When a create refuses, leave it: that refusal is the day's event. When halted or parked, say why and stop. When the Stop hook asks you to close the sitting, make that one call; the note names the day and each row you created or that refused."
  :scope [{:kind "{{kinds}}" :actions ["create"]}]
  :delivers [{:kind "{{kinds}}" :action "create"}]}}
